<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Batticaloa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Batticaloa is a city on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, built around a large lagoon in the Eastern Province. The Portuguese built a fort there in the 1620s, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> captured in 1638 with the help of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, opening their conquest of the island's coasts. The city has a mainly Tamil-speaking population and was badly hit by the 2004 tsunami.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/batticaloa.jpg" alt="Batticaloa" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Croos · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sea_Fishing,_Batticaloa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Batticaloa lies on the flat coastal plain of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, 111 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trincomalee">Trincomalee</a>, at an average elevation of about 5 metres. Its old town stands on the island of Puliyanthivu in the Batticaloa Lagoon, the largest of the district's three lagoons, which is 56 kilometres long and covers 162 square kilometres. Bridges link the island with the mainland, the largest being the Lady Manning bridge at Kallady. The climate is tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with heavy rains from November to February and an average annual rainfall of about 1,650 millimetres. The lagoon is known as the land of the singing fish, after musical sounds heard there. The name is a Portuguese form of the Tamil Matakkalappu, interpreted as Muddy Swamp or Flat Lagoon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Tamil chronicle Mattakallappu Manmiyam tells that the Mukkuvas, a coastal community with roots in the Tamil country, were the first to migrate to the area and built seven villages. Mukkuvas also served Sinhalese kings as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> force. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples to Kannaki Amman across the Eastern Province are traditionally linked to the king Gajabahu I, who is said to have brought settlers and her worship from the Chera kingdom.</p><p>Portuguese power reached the coast in the early 17th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> admiral Joris van Spilbergen anchored at Batticaloa on 31 May 1602, and found the local chief willing to trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cinnamon">cinnamon</a>. He was then taken to the court of King Rajasinghe at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a>, who welcomed the chance to be rid of the Portuguese. The Portuguese fortified Batticaloa in the 1620s, the fort being built in 1628, and the Dutch captured it on 18 May 1638, the first of the Portuguese forts to fall to them. It stands on Puliyanthivu and is regarded as one of the most picturesque of the small Dutch forts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>. The first Catholic church in Batticaloa was established in 1624.</p><p>In 1814 the Wesleyan Methodist William Ault founded Methodist Central College, the town's first educational institution. In 1942 the aircraft carrier HMS Hermes and the destroyer HMAS Vampire were sunk by Japanese aircraft off Batticaloa. The tsunami of 26 December 2004 hit the town severely, with water rising 4.7 metres within 90 minutes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Batticaloa is the administrative capital of the Batticaloa District and a commercial centre of the Eastern Province. Its people are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lankan-tamils">Sri Lankan Tamils</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a>, Sinhalese, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">Burghers</a> and the indigenous Vedda. The town has long been a centre of Portuguese Burgher culture, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is its major religion, with Muslim and Christian communities beside it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Batticaloa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Dutch took <b>Batticaloa</b> in 1638, Trincomalee in 1639 and Galle in 1640, then Colombo in 1656 and Jaffna in 1658, but kept the conquered coasts for themselves rather than handing them to Kandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-ceylon">Dutch Ceylon</a></li></ul></section>
